4694
04:50:23.188 --> 04:50:25.038
What you have is a clash between

4695
04:50:26.028 --> 04:50:29.848
finding a solution to the problem of climate change.

4696
04:50:29.918 --> 04:50:33.318
And what Norman Kirk said when he was Prime Minister was what was important was

4697
04:50:33.348 --> 04:50:37.308
looking for a solution to a problem that would work, rather than arguing about the

4698
04:50:37.348 --> 04:50:37.768
problem.

4699
04:50:38.608 --> 04:50:42.488
So, the solution here is between a parliamentary process, a political

4700
04:50:42.608 --> 04:50:43.188
solution,

4701
04:50:44.128 --> 04:50:47.928
and it's an alternative solution that might rest in a court process.

4702
04:50:48.008 --> 04:50:51.248
And I think it's worth knowing what the advantages and disadvantages of them are.

4703
04:50:52.868 --> 04:50:56.788
I can summarize these by reference to two sets of Reith Lectures, and I'll just

4704
04:50:56.888 --> 04:51:00.668
read briefly some excerpts from Lord Sumption's Reith Lectures in

4705
04:51:00.828 --> 04:51:04.348
2019, where he made a couple of prescient points

4706
04:51:04.368 --> 04:51:06.728
which I think have to be considered here.

4707
04:51:08.808 --> 04:51:12.748
Before I turn to that, the way I'm seeing it is litigants seem to have a

4708
04:51:12.808 --> 04:51:16.768
great deal of expectation of what can be achieved out of a court

4709
04:51:16.788 --> 04:51:20.248
proceeding and tort to solve a global problem.

4710
04:51:21.568 --> 04:51:25.188
It makes no sense, and in my submission and the Law

4711
04:51:25.248 --> 04:51:28.708
Association's position is that Parliament can

4712
04:51:28.728 --> 04:51:32.498
legislate to solve this problem, and it can

4713
04:51:32.528 --> 04:51:36.168
exclude the courts from involvement in it as a matter of power.

4714
04:51:36.188 --> 04:51:39.768
The question is: should it do so? And these are extraordinary

4715
04:51:39.848 --> 04:51:43.748
measures here, depriving litigants of any rights to

4716
04:51:43.888 --> 04:51:44.908
access the courts.

4717
04:51:47.608 --> 04:51:51.208
Some things Lord Sumption said in his Reith Lecture in

4718
04:51:51.348 --> 04:51:54.888
2019 are regarded as inherently illegitimate.

4719
04:51:54.928 --> 04:51:57.258
For example, retrospective legislation,

4720
04:51:58.348 --> 04:52:00.188
obstructing access to a court,

4721
04:52:01.668 --> 04:52:04.068
acts contrary to international law and so on.

4722
04:52:04.468 --> 04:52:06.848
This does not mean that Parliament cannot do them.

4723
04:52:07.328 --> 04:52:11.128
It means that those who propose such things must squarely declare what they are

4724
04:52:11.168 --> 04:52:13.168
doing and take the political heat.

4725
04:52:15.348 --> 04:52:19.308
On the other hand, he went on to address the limitations of what

4726
04:52:19.348 --> 04:52:21.708
can be achieved in proceedings before a court.

4727
04:52:22.788 --> 04:52:24.988
The judicial resolution of policy issues

4728
04:52:25.828 --> 04:52:29.788
can undermine the single biggest advantage of the political process, which

4729
04:52:29.848 --> 04:52:33.128
is to accommodate the divergent interests and opinions of citizens.

4730
04:52:33.448 --> 04:52:35.928
Namely, this is my interleafing, trade-offs.

4731
04:52:36.848 --> 04:52:40.308
It is true that politics do not always perform that function well.

4732
04:52:40.748 --> 04:52:43.968
It may be that politics are not performing it well at the moment.

4733
04:52:45.588 --> 04:52:47.548
But judges will never be able to perform it.

4734
04:52:48.528 --> 04:52:50.868
Litigation can rarely mediate differences.

4735
04:52:51.248 --> 04:52:52.828
It is a zero-sum game.

4736
04:52:53.648 --> 04:52:56.628
The winners carry off the prize and the losers pay.

4737
04:52:57.520 --> 04:53:01.060
It is not a consultative or participatory

4738
04:53:01.160 --> 04:53:01.700
process,

4739
04:53:02.540 --> 04:53:04.000
it is an appeal to law.

4740
04:53:07.200 --> 04:53:11.040
The overarching message of the Law Association is, yes, Parliament

4741
04:53:11.160 --> 04:53:14.880
can do this, but it's far-reaching, it's unprecedented for the

4742
04:53:14.920 --> 04:53:18.640
reasons I've read out from Lord Sumption, and Parliament

4743
04:53:18.680 --> 04:53:22.200
must pause for considerable thought before

4744
04:53:22.500 --> 04:53:24.880
taking the steps that are proposed in the bill.

4745
04:53:27.320 --> 04:53:30.840
The retrospectivity and the

4746
04:53:30.880 --> 04:53:34.800
extinguishment of rights to access justice are particularly important

4747
04:53:34.860 --> 04:53:35.890
features to consider.

4748
04:53:37.270 --> 04:53:39.120
The association has suggested

4749
04:53:40.100 --> 04:53:41.000
an alternative,

4750
04:53:42.220 --> 04:53:44.260
that what could be legislated would

4751
