but give defendants, such as in the Smith and Fonterra case,

4753
04:53:52.780 --> 04:53:56.380
a complete defense to claims if they can show compliance with the statutory

4754
04:53:56.500 --> 04:53:57.800
climate change regime.

4755
04:53:59.260 --> 04:54:02.740
Another option would be to preserve the right to sue in tort

4756
04:54:03.560 --> 04:54:06.940
for emissions that are non-compliant with the statutory

4757
04:54:07.040 --> 04:54:07.580
regime.

4758
04:54:08.860 --> 04:54:12.660
And I want to give an example from my own practice career a long

4759
04:54:12.760 --> 04:54:16.580
time ago, where I had a client who lived behind a timber treatment

4760
04:54:16.660 --> 04:54:19.800
plant that leaked chemicals repeatedly into their section,

4761
04:54:20.720 --> 04:54:22.060
ruined the value of their property.

4762
04:54:23.460 --> 04:54:26.940
The regulator took no steps over many years to enforce the

4763
04:54:26.980 --> 04:54:30.420
regulations to prevent the discharge of these chemicals.

4764
04:54:31.120 --> 04:54:34.260
They were able to sue and obtain a settlement nuisance.

4765
04:54:35.740 --> 04:54:37.140
Now, that shows you the value.

4766
04:54:38.420 --> 04:54:41.800
What we have here is we're taking away the rights of all

4767
04:54:41.860 --> 04:54:45.650
citizens to sue people who are causing a nuisance,

4768
04:54:46.140 --> 04:54:49.180
and to take any steps to make the regulator do the right thing.

4769
04:54:49.920 --> 04:54:52.060
And in the Law Association submission,

4770
04:54:53.020 --> 04:54:56.790
that is a very far-reaching step that Parliament should carefully think

